## How to write a flow here

`/generate-walkthrough` produces a stakeholder demo by *executing* the flows below. That only works
if each step names **a screen a reader can find and the single action that advances it**. Prose like
"the user completes onboarding" cannot be executed, screenshotted, or tested — it reads fine and is
useless to every consumer of this document.

So each step needs three things:

| Needs | Not |
|---|---|
| The screen or route it happens on | "the onboarding section" |
| The one action that advances it — tap, submit, select | "the user provides their details" |
| What makes it end — success, and every failure a user can actually hit | only the happy path |

Number top-level sections, and give each distinct sub-flow its own `###`. Both are cited by name
elsewhere: audit findings and PR descriptions reference flows by number, and the `###` boundary is
what makes a sub-flow addressable on its own.

