## Routing decisions

Distinct from the flows above: flows are what the user experiences, this is what the code enforces.
Keep them apart — mixing them produces a document where you cannot tell an intention from a
guarantee.

Record, with the reasoning:

- **Guards and redirects** — which conditions send a user somewhere other than where they asked to
  go. This is the section that explains surprising navigation a year later.
- **Relaunch behaviour** — where a returning user lands, and what state decides it.
- **Deep links** — which routes are addressable from outside the app, and what happens when one is
  opened without the preconditions its flow assumes.

<!-- DECIDE: does this project support deep links at all? If not, say so — it is a real constraint
     on every flow above, and an absent answer gets rediscovered as a bug report. -->

## State machines

Where an entity moves through statuses — an order, a booking, a request — document the states and
the legal transitions. QA checklists are derived from these, so an undocumented state is an untested
state.

| Entity | States | Legal transitions | Who triggers each |
|---|---|---|---|
| <!-- DECIDE: which entities have a lifecycle worth pinning down? Not everything does; a table of
       trivial state machines dilutes the ones that matter. --> | | | |

Name the states exactly as the code names them. A document calling it `cancelled` while the code
says `canceled` costs someone an afternoon.

